        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "c9f45b28-79bf-511c-b231-8af766ebd40c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: New York, Lake Champlain, Hudson River, southwestern Ohio, Warren County, Lebanon, Middletown, Glens Falls\n- Systems: Champlain Canal, Warren County Canal, Miami and Erie Canal, Glens Falls Feeder Canal\n\nFacts:\n- The Champlain Canal is a 60 mi canal in New York that connects the south end of Lake Champlain to the Hudson River.\n- In the mid-19th century, the Warren County Canal was a branch of the Miami and Erie Canal in southwestern Ohio.\n- The Warren County Canal was about 20 mi long and connected Lebanon, the Warren County seat, to the main canal at Middletown.\n- The Glens Falls Feeder Canal is 7 mi long.\n- The Glens Falls Feeder Canal delivers water from above Glens Falls on the Hudson River to the highest point of the Champlain Canal.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
